//! Growable, generic, contiguous list (`List<T>`).
//!
//! `List<T>` is Nova's dynamic array: the everyday "resizable vector" that most
//! standard-library and application code reaches for. It stores its elements in
//! one contiguous run so that indexing is O(1) and iteration is cache-friendly,
//! and it grows automatically as elements are pushed. The type is generic and
//! monomorphised, so `List<int>` and `List<string>` compile to distinct,
//! specialised code with no boxing or type erasure.
//!
//! All the real storage work lives one layer down in [`RawBuffer`], imported
//! from `mem.rawbuffer`. `List<T>` is deliberately a THIN wrapper over that
//! buffer: `RawBuffer` owns the allocation, the element slots, the growth
//! policy (capacity doubling), and the per-element ARC retain and release when
//! `T` is a heap type. This split keeps the low-level, unsafe pointer
//! arithmetic in one audited place and lets `List` be a small, readable set of
//! ergonomic operations (map, filter, reduce, sort, and friends) written on top
//! of a handful of primitives (`push`, `at`, `set`, `count`, `swap`).
//!
//! Ownership and value semantics: a `List<T>` OWNS its elements. When the list
//! is destroyed the backing buffer releases each element, and when the buffer
//! reallocates on growth the existing elements are moved, not copied and
//! retained twice. Because `List` is a struct with a container field, copying a
//! list value performs a genuine element-by-element copy via [`List.copy`] and
//! [`List.slice`] rather than aliasing the same buffer, which keeps the value
//! semantics that the rest of the language expects from a struct.
//!
//! Two error conventions live side by side here, and the choice is intentional.
//! The lookups that a caller is expected to probe speculatively return an
//! optional (`T | undefined`): [`List.get`], [`List.first`], [`List.last`], and
//! [`List.pop`] hand back `undefined` for an out-of-range index or an empty
//! list. The lookups that assert a precondition instead panic on violation:
//! [`List.at`] calls `nova_panic` when the index is out of bounds, because
//! reaching it with a bad index is a program bug, not a data condition. A few
//! mutators ([`List.set`]) simply no-op on an out-of-range index rather than
//! panic, so a stray write cannot corrupt the buffer.
//!
//! Sorting is an in-place heapsort ([`List.sort`] and [`List.sortBy`]), driven
//! by the private sift-down helpers [`List.heapSiftDown`] and
//! [`List.heapSiftDownBy`]. Heapsort is chosen for its O(n log n) worst case and
//! its lack of extra allocation: it rearranges the existing buffer through
//! `swap` alone. It is not a stable sort, so callers that need stability must
//! encode a tiebreak into their comparator or key.

import mem.rawbuffer;

/// A growable, contiguous, owning sequence of `T` backed by a [`RawBuffer`].
///
/// The element storage, capacity growth, and per-element ARC are all delegated
/// to the [`List.data`] buffer; the struct itself only adds the higher-level
/// list operations and two cached counters. Prefer the constructor `List<T>()`
/// (the `init` below) which starts with a small non-zero capacity so the first
/// few pushes do not reallocate.
pub struct List<T> {
    /// The backing store that actually owns the elements and the allocation.
    ///
    /// Every element access, mutation, growth, and destruction routes through
    /// this buffer. It is the single source of truth for the real element
    /// count: the [`List.len`] and [`List.cap`] fields are only cached mirrors
    /// of `data.count()` and `data.capacity()`, and the methods here read
    /// `data.count()` directly rather than trusting the cached `len`.
    pub data: RawBuffer<T>,
    /// Cached copy of the element count, refreshed after size-changing calls.
    ///
    /// This mirrors `data.count()` for cheap external inspection, but it is NOT
    /// the authoritative length: the read-only operations below query
    /// `self.data.count()` directly, and only the mutators ([`List.push`],
    /// [`List.insert`], [`List.remove`], [`List.pop`], [`List.clear`]) write
    /// this field back. Do not rely on it inside a method that has mutated the
    /// buffer without also refreshing it.
    pub len: int,
    /// Cached copy of the backing buffer's allocated capacity.
    ///
    /// Mirrors `data.capacity()` and is updated by the same mutators that touch
    /// [`List.len`]. It reflects how many elements can be held before the next
    /// reallocation, not the current element count.
    pub cap: int,

    /// Creates an empty list with a small initial capacity of four slots.
    ///
    /// Starting non-empty avoids a reallocation on each of the first few
    /// pushes. The buffer is allocated eagerly, so a freshly constructed list
    /// already owns storage even though [`List.len`] is zero.
    init() {
        self.data = rawbuffer.RawBuffer<T>(4);
        self.len = 0;
        self.cap = 4;
    }

    /// Appends `value` to the end of the list, growing the buffer if needed.
    ///
    /// The append itself is delegated to [`List.data`]; the buffer may reallocate
    /// (typically doubling capacity) when it is full, which is why the cached
    /// [`List.len`] and [`List.cap`] are re-read from the buffer afterwards.
    /// Amortised O(1).
    pub fn push(self: List<T>, value: T): void {
        self.data.push(value);
        self.len = self.data.count();
        self.cap = self.data.capacity();
    }

    /// Returns the element at `index`, or `undefined` if the index is out of range.
    ///
    /// This is the safe, probe-friendly lookup: a negative index or one at or
    /// beyond the element count yields `undefined` rather than panicking. Use
    /// this when an out-of-range index is an ordinary, expected outcome; use
    /// [`List.at`] when a bad index would be a program bug.
    pub fn get(self: List<T>, index: int): T | undefined {
        if (index < 0 || index >= self.data.count()) {
            return undefined;
        }
        return self.data.at(index);
    }

    /// Returns the element at `index`, panicking if the index is out of range.
    ///
    /// This is the assertive lookup: it calls `nova_panic` on a negative index
    /// or one at or beyond the count, on the understanding that reaching it with
    /// a bad index means the caller's own invariant is broken. When an
    /// out-of-range index is a normal data condition, prefer [`List.get`], which
    /// returns `undefined` instead.
    pub fn at(self: List<T>, index: int): T {
        if (index < 0 || index >= self.data.count()) {
            nova_panic("List.at: index out of bounds");
        }
        return self.data.at(index);
    }

    /// Overwrites the element at `index` with `value`, ignoring out-of-range indices.
    ///
    /// Unlike [`List.at`] this neither panics nor grows the list: a negative or
    /// past-the-end index is silently a no-op, so a stray write cannot corrupt
    /// the buffer or extend it. It replaces an existing slot only, and the
    /// buffer releases the old occupant as part of the set.
    pub fn set(self: List<T>, index: int, value: T): void {
        if (index < 0 || index >= self.data.count()) {
            return;
        }
        self.data.set(index, value);
    }

    /// Inserts `value` at `index`, shifting later elements one slot to the right.
    ///
    /// Delegates the shift and any growth to [`List.data`], then refreshes the
    /// cached [`List.len`] and [`List.cap`]. Because every element from `index`
    /// onwards moves up by one, this is O(n) in the number of trailing
    /// elements. Bounds handling follows the buffer's `insertAt` contract.
    pub fn insert(self: List<T>, index: int, value: T): void {
        self.data.insertAt(index, value);
        self.len = self.data.count();
        self.cap = self.data.capacity();
    }

    /// Removes the element at `index`, shifting later elements one slot to the left.
    ///
    /// The removed element is released by the buffer and the gap is closed, so
    /// this is O(n) in the number of trailing elements. The cached [`List.len`]
    /// and [`List.cap`] are refreshed from the buffer afterwards. Out-of-range
    /// handling follows the buffer's `removeAt` contract.
    pub fn remove(self: List<T>, index: int): void {
        self.data.removeAt(index);
        self.len = self.data.count();
        self.cap = self.data.capacity();
    }

    /// Returns the current number of elements.
    ///
    /// Reads the authoritative count straight from [`List.data`] rather than the
    /// cached [`List.len`], so it is always correct even immediately after a
    /// buffer mutation.
    pub fn size(self: List<T>): int {
        return self.data.count();
    }

    /// Applies `fn` to each element in order, front to back, for its side effects.
    ///
    /// The count is re-read each iteration, so this walks the list as it stands.
    /// It does not build or return anything; for a transforming pass that yields
    /// a new list use [`List.map`] instead.
    pub fn forEach(self: List<T>, fn: (T) -> void): void {
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.data.count()) {
            let val = self.data.at(i);
            fn(val);
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    /// Builds a new `List<U>` by applying `fn` to every element in order.
    ///
    /// The source list is left unchanged. The result is a freshly owned list of
    /// the same length, in the same order. This is the transforming counterpart
    /// to [`List.forEach`].
    pub fn map<U>(self: List<T>, fn: (T) -> U): List<U> {
        let result = List<U>();
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.data.count()) {
            let val = self.data.at(i);
            let mapped = fn(val);
            result.push(mapped);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return result;
    }

    /// Builds a new list holding only the elements for which `pred` returns true.
    ///
    /// Order is preserved and the source list is untouched. The result may be
    /// empty (if nothing matches) or as long as the source (if everything does).
    pub fn filter(self: List<T>, pred: (T) -> bool): List<T> {
        let result = List<T>();
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.data.count()) {
            let val = self.data.at(i);
            if (pred(val)) {
                result.push(val);
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return result;
    }

    /// Folds the list into a single value of type `U`, left to right.
    ///
    /// Starts from the seed `acc` and threads it through `fn(accumulator,
    /// element)` for each element in order, returning the final accumulator. On
    /// an empty list the seed is returned unchanged. This is a left fold; the
    /// combining function receives the running accumulator first.
    pub fn reduce<U>(self: List<T>, acc: U, fn: (U, T) -> U): U {
        let result = acc;
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.data.count()) {
            let val = self.data.at(i);
            result = fn(result, val);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return result;
    }

    /// Reverses the elements in place using a two-pointer swap from both ends.
    ///
    /// Walks one index up from the front and one down from the back, swapping
    /// until they meet, so it does O(n/2) swaps and allocates nothing. Mutates
    /// the list directly rather than returning a new one.
    pub fn reverse(self: List<T>): void {
        let i = 0;
        let j = self.data.count() - 1;
        while (i < j) {
            self.data.swap(i, j);
            i = i + 1;
            j = j - 1;
        }
    }

    /// Empties the list, releasing all elements but keeping the allocation.
    ///
    /// The backing buffer drops its elements and resets its count, so the
    /// capacity is retained for reuse and [`List.cap`] is deliberately not
    /// changed here. Only [`List.len`] is reset to zero.
    pub fn clear(self: List<T>): void {
        self.data.clear();
        self.len = 0;
    }

    /// Returns the first element, or `undefined` if the list is empty.
    ///
    /// A safe peek that never panics. See [`List.last`] for the other end and
    /// [`List.get`] for arbitrary indices.
    pub fn first(self: List<T>): T | undefined {
        if (self.data.count() == 0) {
            return undefined;
        }
        return self.data.at(0);
    }

    /// Returns the last element, or `undefined` if the list is empty.
    ///
    /// A safe peek that never panics. Reads the element at `count - 1`. See
    /// [`List.first`] for the other end.
    pub fn last(self: List<T>): T | undefined {
        if (self.data.count() == 0) {
            return undefined;
        }
        return self.data.at(self.data.count() - 1);
    }

    /// Removes and returns the last element, or `undefined` if the list is empty.
    ///
    /// This is the pairing for [`List.push`]: together they make the list usable
    /// as a stack, both operating at the back in amortised O(1). The cached
    /// [`List.len`] and [`List.cap`] are refreshed from the buffer after the
    /// removal.
    pub fn pop(self: List<T>): T | undefined {
        let v = self.data.pop();
        self.len = self.data.count();
        self.cap = self.data.capacity();
        return v;
    }

    /// Returns the index of the first element satisfying `pred`, or -1 if none do.
    ///
    /// Scans front to back and stops at the first match. The `-1` sentinel is
    /// the "not found" signal shared with [`List.indexOf`]; callers test against
    /// it (as [`List.any`] does) rather than receiving an optional.
    pub fn findIndex(self: List<T>, pred: (T) -> bool): int {
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.data.count()) {
            if (pred(self.data.at(i))) {
                return i;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    /// Returns true if at least one element satisfies `pred`.
    ///
    /// Implemented on top of [`List.findIndex`], so it short-circuits at the
    /// first match. On an empty list it returns false. This is the existential
    /// counterpart to [`List.all`].
    pub fn any(self: List<T>, pred: (T) -> bool): bool {
        return self.findIndex(pred) != -1;
    }

    /// Returns true if every element satisfies `pred`.
    ///
    /// Short-circuits and returns false at the first element that fails. On an
    /// empty list it returns true (vacuous truth), which is the standard
    /// convention and the mirror of [`List.any`] returning false when empty.
    pub fn all(self: List<T>, pred: (T) -> bool): bool {
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.data.count()) {
            if (!pred(self.data.at(i))) {
                return false;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return true;
    }

    /// Returns the index of the first element equal to `value`, or -1 if absent.
    ///
    /// Uses `==` for the comparison, so `T` must support equality. This is the
    /// value-based sibling of [`List.findIndex`] (which takes a predicate) and
    /// backs [`List.contains`].
    pub fn indexOf(self: List<T>, value: T): int {
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.data.count()) {
            if (self.data.at(i) == value) {
                return i;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    /// Returns true if any element equals `value`.
    ///
    /// A membership test built on [`List.indexOf`], so it short-circuits at the
    /// first equal element and relies on `==` for `T`.
    pub fn contains(self: List<T>, value: T): bool {
        return self.indexOf(value) != -1;
    }

    /// Returns an independent shallow copy of the whole list.
    ///
    /// Implemented as a full-range [`List.slice`], so it produces a new list
    /// with its own buffer holding the same elements (each retained as it is
    /// pushed into the copy). Mutating either list afterwards does not affect
    /// the other; the elements themselves are shared by reference when `T` is a
    /// heap type, as usual for a shallow copy.
    pub fn copy(self: List<T>): List<T> {
        return self.slice(0, self.data.count());
    }

    /// Returns a new list holding the half-open range `[start, end)`.
    ///
    /// Both bounds are clamped into `0 .. count` before use, so out-of-range or
    /// inverted arguments cannot panic or read past the buffer: a `start` past
    /// the end, or an `end` below `start`, simply yields an empty list. The
    /// source is left unchanged and the result owns a fresh buffer. This is the
    /// building block for [`List.copy`].
    pub fn slice(self: List<T>, start: int, end: int): List<T> {
        let n = self.data.count();
        let s = if (start < 0) 0 else if (start > n) n else start;
        let e = if (end < 0) 0 else if (end > n) n else end;
        let out = List<T>();
        let i = s;
        while (i < e) {
            out.push(self.data.at(i));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    /// Returns a new list containing this list's elements followed by `other`'s.
    ///
    /// Neither input is mutated; the result is a fresh list of length
    /// `self.size() + other.size()`, in order. Elements are copied in by
    /// pushing, so heap elements are retained into the new buffer.
    pub fn concat(self: List<T>, other: List<T>): List<T> {
        let out = List<T>();
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.data.count()) {
            out.push(self.data.at(i));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        let j = 0;
        while (j < other.size()) {
            out.push(other.at(j));
            j = j + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    /// Restores the max-heap property at subtree `root` within the prefix `[0, n)`.
    ///
    /// This is the private engine of [`List.sort`]. It treats the buffer as a
    /// binary heap (children of `i` at `2i+1` and `2i+2`), finds the largest of
    /// the node and its two in-range children according to `cmp`, and if a child
    /// is larger it swaps and descends into that child, repeating until the node
    /// dominates its children. `cmp(a, b) > 0` means `a` sorts after `b`, so the
    /// element that compares greatest bubbles to the top. Only the first `n`
    /// slots are considered, which is how the sort shrinks the heap region as it
    /// extracts the maximum to the tail.
    fn heapSiftDown(self: List<T>, root: int, n: int, cmp: (T, T) -> int): void {
        let i = root;
        while (true) {
            let largest = i;
            let left = 2 * i + 1;
            let right = 2 * i + 2;
            if (left < n) {
                let el = self.data.at(left);
                let elg = self.data.at(largest);
                if (cmp(el, elg) > 0) { largest = left; }
            }
            if (right < n) {
                let er = self.data.at(right);
                let erg = self.data.at(largest);
                if (cmp(er, erg) > 0) { largest = right; }
            }
            if (largest == i) { return; }
            self.data.swap(i, largest);
            i = largest;
        }
    }

    /// Sorts the list in place into ascending order according to `cmp`.
    ///
    /// The comparator returns a negative number when its first argument sorts
    /// before its second, zero when they are equivalent, and a positive number
    /// when it sorts after, the usual three-way convention. This is an in-place
    /// heapsort: it first builds a max-heap by sifting down from the last
    /// internal node, then repeatedly swaps the maximum at index 0 to the end of
    /// the shrinking heap region and re-sifts. Worst and average case are both
    /// O(n log n) and no extra buffer is allocated. Lists of length below two
    /// are already sorted and return immediately. Note this sort is NOT stable:
    /// equal elements may be reordered, so encode a tiebreak into `cmp` if order
    /// among equals matters. See [`List.sortBy`] for a key-function variant and
    /// [`List.heapSiftDown`] for the underlying step.
    pub fn sort(self: List<T>, cmp: (T, T) -> int): void {
        let n = self.data.count();
        if (n < 2) { return; }
        let start = n / 2 - 1;
        while (start >= 0) { self.heapSiftDown(start, n, cmp); start = start - 1; }
        let end = n - 1;
        while (end > 0) {
            self.data.swap(0, end);
            self.heapSiftDown(0, end, cmp);
            end = end - 1;
        }
    }

    /// Returns a new list with the first `n` elements (fewer if the list is shorter).
    ///
    /// The count is clamped into `0 .. size()`, so a negative `n` yields an empty
    /// list and an `n` larger than the list yields a full copy. The source is
    /// unchanged. This is the complement of [`List.drop`]: `take(n)` ++
    /// `drop(n)` reconstructs the original order.
    pub fn take(self: List<T>, n: int): List<T> {
        let out = List<T>();
        let count = self.data.count();
        let lim = n;
        if (lim < 0) {
            lim = 0;
        }
        if (lim > count) {
            lim = count;
        }
        let i = 0;
        while (i < lim) {
            out.push(self.data.at(i));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    /// Returns a new list with the first `n` elements removed.
    ///
    /// The skip count is clamped into `0 .. size()`, so a negative `n` keeps
    /// everything and an `n` past the end yields an empty list. The source is
    /// unchanged. Complement of [`List.take`].
    pub fn drop(self: List<T>, n: int): List<T> {
        let out = List<T>();
        let count = self.data.count();
        let start = n;
        if (start < 0) {
            start = 0;
        }
        if (start > count) {
            start = count;
        }
        let i = start;
        while (i < count) {
            out.push(self.data.at(i));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    /// Returns how many elements satisfy `pred`.
    ///
    /// Walks the whole list and tallies the matches, so it does not
    /// short-circuit the way [`List.any`] does. Note the name is overloaded in
    /// spirit with the buffer's element count, but this method takes a predicate
    /// and counts matches, whereas plain size comes from [`List.size`].
    pub fn count(self: List<T>, pred: (T) -> bool): int {
        let c = 0;
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.data.count()) {
            if (pred(self.data.at(i))) {
                c = c + 1;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return c;
    }

    /// Maps each element to a `List<U>` and concatenates the results in order.
    ///
    /// For every element `fn` produces a sublist, and each sublist's elements
    /// are appended, front to back, into one flat result. This is [`List.map`]
    /// followed by a flatten in a single pass; an element mapping to an empty
    /// list contributes nothing.
    pub fn flatMap<U>(self: List<T>, fn: (T) -> List<U>): List<U> {
        let out = List<U>();
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.data.count()) {
            let sub = fn(self.data.at(i));
            let j = 0;
            while (j < sub.size()) {
                out.push(sub.at(j));
                j = j + 1;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    /// Splits the list into consecutive sublists of at most `size` elements each.
    ///
    /// Elements are taken in order, so the result reads left to right and only
    /// the final chunk may be shorter than `size`. A `size` of zero or less is
    /// treated as invalid and yields an empty result rather than looping, which
    /// also avoids a division or non-terminating step. The source is unchanged.
    pub fn chunk(self: List<T>, size: int): List<List<T>> {
        let out = List<List<T>>();
        if (size <= 0) {
            return out;
        }
        let count = self.data.count();
        let i = 0;
        while (i < count) {
            let piece = List<T>();
            let j = 0;
            while (j < size && i + j < count) {
                piece.push(self.data.at(i + j));
                j = j + 1;
            }
            out.push(piece);
            i = i + size;
        }
        return out;
    }

    /// Pairs elements of this list with `other` positionally into a list of tuples.
    ///
    /// The result has length equal to the SHORTER of the two lists; any trailing
    /// elements of the longer list are dropped. Element `i` of the result is the
    /// tuple `(self[i], other[i])`. Neither input is mutated.
    pub fn zip<U>(self: List<T>, other: List<U>): List<(T, U)> {
        let out = List<(T, U)>();
        let n = self.data.count();
        let m = other.size();
        let lim = n;
        if (m < lim) {
            lim = m;
        }
        let i = 0;
        while (i < lim) {
            out.push((self.data.at(i), other.at(i)));
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }

    /// Sift-down step of [`List.sortBy`], ordering by an integer key rather than a comparator.
    ///
    /// Identical in structure to [`List.heapSiftDown`] but it compares
    /// `key(child) > key(parent)` instead of calling a three-way comparator, so
    /// the element with the greatest key bubbles up. Restores the max-heap
    /// property at `root` within the prefix `[0, n)`. Note it recomputes `key`
    /// on each comparison, so an expensive key function is evaluated repeatedly.
    fn heapSiftDownBy(self: List<T>, root: int, n: int, key: (T) -> int): void {
        let i = root;
        while (true) {
            let largest = i;
            let left = 2 * i + 1;
            let right = 2 * i + 2;
            if (left < n) {
                let el = self.data.at(left);
                let elg = self.data.at(largest);
                if (key(el) > key(elg)) { largest = left; }
            }
            if (right < n) {
                let er = self.data.at(right);
                let erg = self.data.at(largest);
                if (key(er) > key(erg)) { largest = right; }
            }
            if (largest == i) { return; }
            self.data.swap(i, largest);
            i = largest;
        }
    }

    /// Sorts the list in place into ascending order of the integer `key`.
    ///
    /// A convenience over [`List.sort`] for the common case of ordering by a
    /// numeric field: instead of writing a three-way comparator you supply a
    /// function that projects each element to an `int`, and elements are ordered
    /// by that projection ascending. Same in-place heapsort characteristics as
    /// [`List.sort`]: O(n log n), no extra allocation, and NOT stable. The key
    /// is recomputed during sifting via [`List.heapSiftDownBy`], so keep it
    /// cheap. Lists shorter than two elements return immediately.
    pub fn sortBy(self: List<T>, key: (T) -> int): void {
        let n = self.data.count();
        if (n < 2) { return; }
        let start = n / 2 - 1;
        while (start >= 0) { self.heapSiftDownBy(start, n, key); start = start - 1; }
        let end = n - 1;
        while (end > 0) {
            self.data.swap(0, end);
            self.heapSiftDownBy(0, end, key);
            end = end - 1;
        }
    }
}

/// Smoke test for the core list lifecycle: construct, push, size, and index.
///
/// Run by `nova test`, it checks that a new list starts empty, that pushes grow
/// the reported size, and that positional [`List.get`] returns the pushed
/// values. It is a minimal guard on the buffer wiring rather than a full
/// coverage suite, and it reports failures through `nova_test_fail`.
@test
fn test_list(): void {
    let list = List<int>();
    if (list.size() != 0) {
        nova_test_fail("list.size != 0");
    }
    list.push(10);
    list.push(20);
    list.push(30);
    if (list.size() != 3) {
        nova_test_fail("list.size != 3");
    }
    if (list.get(0) != 10) {
        nova_test_fail("list[0] != 10");
    }
}
